@page "/admin"
@attribute [Authorize]
@inject AdminAccess Admin

@*
	The administration screens' front door (§14.6).

	Three destinations and nothing else on it, in the same card shape Settings uses, because
	it is reached the same way and by the same person — the entry on Settings is the only
	link to it anywhere in the app.

	**Not on the nav rail.** The rail is five destinations for what a rider opens twice in a
	ride with gloves on; this is a thing somebody opens at a desk when a question has come
	up, and it is a thing most accounts must never see at all. See NavMenu's own note on why
	that list does not grow.

	The gate here is a courtesy, not the control. It stops an administrator's browser
	offering three screens that would only ever answer 403, and it is checked again — against
	the server's own roster, per request — by everything behind it.
*@

<PageTitle>Administration — Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

<div class="page page-medium">
	<PageNav Title="Administration" BackHref="/settings" BackLabel="Back to settings" />

	@if (_allowed is null)
	{
		<p class="empty">Loading…</p>
	}
	else if (_allowed is false)
	{
		<p class="error">
			This account is not an administrator of this server. Administrators are named in the
			server's own configuration — there is nothing to switch on from in here.
		</p>
	}
	else
	{
		<div class="cards">
			<a class="card" href="/admin/users">
				<h2><i class="fa fa-fw fa-users" aria-hidden="true"></i> Travellers</h2>
				<p>Every account, when it was last heard from, and what it has put into the service.</p>
			</a>

			<a class="card" href="/admin/stats">
				<h2><i class="fa fa-fw fa-chart-line" aria-hidden="true"></i> Statistics</h2>
				<p>Who is active, what is live right now, and GPS fixes per minute over the day.</p>
			</a>

			<a class="card" href="/admin/logs">
				<h2><i class="fa fa-fw fa-file-lines" aria-hidden="true"></i> Server log</h2>
				<p>What the server has been doing. Not this device's log — that one is under Settings.</p>
			</a>
		</div>
	}
</div>

@code {
	/// <summary>
	/// Whether this account may be here. Null while the answer is still being fetched, which is
	/// drawn as "loading" rather than as "no" — flashing a refusal at an administrator on every
	/// visit would teach them to ignore it.
	/// </summary>
	private bool? _allowed;

	protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => _allowed = await Admin.IsAdminAsync();
}
